AB  - This paper aims to examine how government management of Covid-19 affects voter turnout in presidential elections among poor people in Africa. The analysis is based on a sample of 17,719 people living in 34 African countries. The econometric results, obtained using a recursive bivariate probit model, show that poverty significantly increases citizens’ willingness to participate in presidential elections. However, this positive influence of poverty changes with improved government management of the Covid-19 pandemic. Indeed, the probability of participating in the presidential election among the poor decreases significantly with (i) the equitable distribution of humanitarian aid against Covid-19, (ii) the decrease in corruption associated with the Covid-19 pandemic, and (iii) the increase in public investment in health. © 2025 The Korean Association for Public Administration.
