UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010941574?origin=resultslist
AB  - This study investigates the factors influencing tourist arrivals in 44 African countries from 1996 to 2017. It stands out by using a Bayesian model averaging estimation technique, which helps address the model uncertainty that has been a limitation in previous research. This method enables the consideration of a wide range of potential factors, both fundamental and proximate, that may affect tourist arrivals. The key findings reveal the following fundamental factors, listed in order of importance: political globalization, population size, literacy rates, GDP per capita, economic globalization, cultural globalization, anocracy, political stability, gross fixed capital formation, and control of corruption. In contrast, the factors that hinder tourism include being landlocked, insufficient infrastructure, strong real exchange rates, inflation, malaria prevalence, and social globalization. Furthermore, the study highlights that government expenditure and language diversity, as proximate factors, follow different patterns. It also notes that the determinants of tourist arrivals vary across regions within Africa. In conclusion, the study underscores that to unlock the tourism sector’s potential in African economies, it is crucial to understand both the drivers and barriers to tourist arrivals, considering both continental and regional perspectives. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
